Code: Fast-BVH, an optimized Bounding Volume Hierarchy
Author: Brandon Pelfrey (brandonpelfrey@gmail.com)
        Borrowed AABB Intersection code referenced in BBox.cpp
Date: April 17, 2012

This code, which I am releasing for public use, provides an optimized Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH).
The vector and axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) code included requires SSE in order to function.
The entire build and intersection test functionality is recursion-less and is much more optimized than
the one found in say, PBRT (Physically Based Rendering, by Pharr + Humphreys). With that said, if you
see another cool trick to speed things up, I'd love to see it!

The BVH itself makes minimal assumptions on the items it may contain: Objects need only implement the 
getIntersection(), getBBox(), and getCentroid() functions in order to be queryable.
The included example code renders a million spheres distributed inside of a cube using the BVH (in <3 seconds on my old MacBook!)   

I have attempted to comment this code well and make everything well-understood. If there is an issue 
with the code, a question as to how something works, or if you use this in a project, please let me know!

================================================================================
Hierarchical Scene Graph (FastBVH::SceneGraph)
================================================================================

Header: include/FastBVH/SceneGraph.h (also pulled in by include/FastBVH.h).
Everything is header-only, uses double and the C++14 standard library, and
lives in namespace FastBVH::SceneGraph. It builds a directed acyclic graph on
top of the existing single-level BVH: a group references meshes or other
groups through affine edges, and the same child may have many parents.
Shared children are stored once and are never expanded into per-instance
copies of the tree or the world-space triangles.

Types
-----
  NodeId       uint32_t stable handle returned by add_mesh / add_group.
  Triangle     Vec3 a, b, c plus uint32_t material. Winding a->b->c defines
               the front face; vertices must be finite and non-degenerate.
  Edge         uint32_t id; NodeId child; std::array<double,12> transform
               (row-major 3x4 affine); uint32_t mask; int64_t
               material_override (-1 = no override, a non-negative value that
               fits in uint32_t overrides the face material).
  Hit          std::vector<uint32_t> path (edge ids root->mesh); NodeId mesh;
               uint32_t primitive_id, material; double t; Vec3 position,
               normal. normal is unit length and follows the actual world
               winding (mirroring flips it). primitive_id is the triangle's
               index in the original add_mesh input regardless of BVH order.
  QueryStats   uint64_t node_visits, edge_tests, triangle_tests. Zeroed at
               the start of every call.
  StorageStats uint64_t mesh_count, group_count, stored_edges,
               stored_triangles. Counts owned objects once, never per path.

Scene construction
------------------
  NodeId add_mesh(std::vector<Triangle>);
  NodeId add_group(std::vector<Edge>);
Both take the container by value and move it, so the Scene owns its data and
the caller's container may be destroyed afterwards. Empty meshes and empty
groups are legal and get empty (no-op) BVHs. Every child edge must reference
an existing node, edge ids must be unique within a group, and each transform
must be finite with a non-zero (hence invertible) determinant. Any invalid
add throws std::invalid_argument and allocates no node.

Queries
-------
  bool closest(NodeId root, const Ray<double>&, double tmin, double tmax,
               uint32_t mask, Hit&, QueryStats* = nullptr);
  bool occluded(NodeId root, const Ray<double>&, double tmin, double tmax,
                uint32_t mask, QueryStats* = nullptr);
The distance window is the finite closed interval 0 <= tmin <= tmax. The ray
direction must be finite and non-zero but need not be unit length; it is
never normalized, so t is always the original world parameter. Intersections
are double sided. closest leaves its output Hit untouched on a miss. Exact
distance ties are resolved by the lexicographically smallest
(path, primitive_id). Along a path the effective mask is the query mask
AND-ed with every edge mask, and the first non-negative material override on
the root-to-leaf path wins, otherwise the triangle material is used. Each
group and mesh is traversed through its own BVH (node pruning), never by
line-scanning edges or faces. All path-dependent state (mask, override,
path, transforms) lives on the traversal stack, so multiple read-only
queries may run concurrently.

Frame updates
-------------
  bool replace_edges(NodeId group, std::vector<Edge>);
Intended for inter-frame edits. On any invalid input (unknown group, mesh
handle, duplicate edge id, non-finite/singular transform, out-of-range
override, unknown child, or a cycle including an indirect one) it returns
false and changes nothing: old edges, bounds, query results and storage
counters are preserved. On success the group identity is unchanged and the
new edges are queryable immediately with no caller-side rebuild. Only the
replaced group's edge BVH is rebuilt; every still-dependent shared ancestor
keeps its node connections and edge ordering and is refitted bottom-up in
dependency order via the new native BVH::refit(BoxConverter) (an empty tree
is a no-op). Unrelated sub-groups and mesh BLAS are not rebuilt. Groups and
roots may be emptied and later refilled; removing edges drops their old
reverse dependencies, so no stale parent relationship survives.

Diagnostics
-----------
  GroupBvhDiagnostic diagnose_group_bvh(NodeId group);
  StorageStats storage_stats();
The diagnostic snapshots the flattened group BVH nodes (bounding box,
primitive start/count, right offset, leaf flag) and the concatenated leaf
edge ids, so node connections and edge-id ordering can be compared across
replace_edges calls (bounds themselves change during a refit).
storage_stats counts objects the Scene actually owns; a mesh or group
referenced through many parent paths is still counted once.

The original DefaultBuilder, BVH and Traverser remain unchanged and source
compatible; BVH gains only the additional refit() template method. No
external models or third-party dependencies are used.
